  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Access to Basic Needs, Protection and Transportation Services for Refugees in Jordan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Refugees account for almost ten per cent of Jordan&#x2019;s population. There are 659,941 registered Syrian refugees and roughly 80,735 registered refugees of other nationalities, primarily from Iraq, Yemen, and Sudan. The vast majority reside outside camps in urban areas, though they face a higher incidence, depth and severity of poverty that led to negative coping strategies ranging from stress coping to crisis and emergency coping methods such as child labour and reduction of essential health and education costs to meet basic needs. Moreover, over 48,000 Syrian refugees have returned to Syria spontaneously since October 2018, with a continued need for transportation assistance to the border and for family reunification. 
 
The International Organization for Migration (IOM) is providing multi-purpose cash assistance to the most vulnerable refugees in Jordan to help them meet their immediate basic needs. IOM is also providing integrated case management to assist at least 480 individual case members to address their protection needs, alongside specialized protection and gender-based violence service providers. Furthermore, IOM is providing transportation assistance to the Jordanian border for small groups of refugees who voluntarily wish to return to Syria as well as support them with transportation for documentation assistance or family reunification.</narrative>
  </description>
